      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Create a synthetic appointment service with nonempty success, empty success, and offline failure; consume it through a protocol with separate success and failure callbacks.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare AppointmentServing with appointments() throws -> [String] and an AppointmentFailure.offline error.",
          "Implement a fake configured with Result\u003c[String], AppointmentFailure>, explaining the generic angle-bracket syntax.",
          "Add a count helper in a protocol extension while preserving throws.",
          "Write a consumer accepting any AppointmentServing plus onSuccess and onFailure callbacks.",
          "Demonstrate all three outcomes and justify whether either callback must escape."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Nonempty and empty arrays both invoke success exactly once.",
          "Offline invokes failure and never masquerades as a zero-count success.",
          "The extension helper preserves errors.",
          "Callbacks run during the synchronous consumer call and need no @escaping.",
          "No real appointments, requests, or production file edits are used."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Result is a generic enum: the first type parameter is successful data and the second is an Error-conforming failure. Its get operation returns success or throws failure. This is a complete standalone answer; keep it separate from earlier declarations.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">protocol AppointmentServing {\n    func appointments() throws -&gt; [String]\n}\nenum AppointmentFailure: Error { case offline }\n\nstruct FakeAppointments: AppointmentServing {\n    let result: Result&lt;[String], AppointmentFailure&gt;\n    func appointments() throws -&gt; [String] { try result.get() }\n}\nextension AppointmentServing {\n    func count() throws -&gt; Int { try appointments().count }\n}\nfunc loadAppointments(\n    using service: any AppointmentServing,\n    onSuccess: ([String]) -&gt; Void,\n    onFailure: (any Error) -&gt; Void\n) {\n    do {\n        onSuccess(try service.appointments())\n    } catch {\n        onFailure(error)\n    }\n}\nlet outcomes: [Result&lt;[String], AppointmentFailure&gt;] = [\n    .success([\"Synthetic visit\"]),\n    .success([]),\n    .failure(.offline)\n]\nfor outcome in outcomes {\n    loadAppointments(\n        using: FakeAppointments(result: outcome),\n        onSuccess: { print(\"Success count:\", $0.count) },\n        onFailure: { print(\"Failure:\", $0) }\n    )\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The two success lines contain counts 1 and 0. The failure line reports offline. The helper and consumer do not silently substitute defaults. Both callbacks are ordinary nonescaping parameters because no code stores or returns them. If you later put onSuccess in a retained handler, change the parameter annotation and review what it captures; adding escaping alone does not establish correct ownership.\u003c/p>"
      },
