        "solution": "\u003cp>The cancellation generated target is ZDAppointmentsCancellation, a static framework for iOS with deployment 17.0. Its source glob selects the active cancellation Sources directory. The test target is ZDAppointmentsCancellationTests, a unit-test bundle with a dependency on the cancellation target. The retained package declares matching target names and tools 5.9.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The lab layout is \u003ccode>Package.swift\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Sources/FoundationLab/Selection.swift\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>Tests/FoundationLabTests/SelectionTests.swift\u003c/code>. The product exposes the library target; tests depend on it and import its module. The synthetic manifest above is the worked configuration. In the next session we will deliberately use a modern tools declaration for Swift Testing, while keeping the same source/test structure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A missing module suggests checking the consuming target's dependency and exported product before editing imports. A UIKit host import failure suggests checking platform/destination. A test that compiles but is not discovered suggests checking source glob, test target, framework conventions, and scheme's test action. These are hypotheses with targeted checks, not guaranteed diagnoses. A successful standalone run validates only the isolated program; it does not validate the entire app graph.\u003c/p>"
