      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Create a miniature review owner that stores a refresh callback, keeps empty success distinct from failure, and permits safe callback invocation after owner release. Add an independent draft-copy prediction and a short build-map note.",
        "steps": [
          "Without the answer, declare an outcome enum, throwing service protocol, and deterministic fake.",
          "Implement a final class with a stored optional refresh callback and state. Choose capture ownership deliberately.",
          "Write an exhaustive summary function that differentiates initial state, no matches, names, and retry.",
          "Save the callback, release its owner, observe through a weak probe, and invoke the saved callback safely.",
          "Demonstrate offline separately; write predicted output before running.",
          "For the earlier ReviewDraft snippet, explain direct field independence and nested sharing. Finish with the active feature's source/test target map."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Empty success renders no matches and offline renders retry.",
          "The stored callback does not form an owner cycle, and post-release invocation is safe.",
          "No force unwrap, try!, deliberate crash, live network, or production edit is required.",
          "All outcome cases are covered and the build note distinguishes tools, mode, and platform.",
          "You submit predictions and actual or paper-traced output, plus two concepts to revisit.",
          "Prior lessons and learner progress are not automatically marked complete."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>This complete standalone implementation uses a weak callback because refresh may be retained after the owner is gone. The fake is a struct; the owner is a class because the exercise intentionally needs reference lifetime. Read it only after drafting your version.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">enum ReviewOutcome: Equatable {\n    case idle\n    case loaded([String])\n    case failed(String)\n}\nenum ReviewFailure: Error { case offline }\nprotocol ReviewServing {\n    func names() throws -&gt; [String]\n}\nstruct ReviewFake: ReviewServing {\n    let result: Result&lt;[String], ReviewFailure&gt;\n    func names() throws -&gt; [String] { try result.get() }\n}\nfinal class ReviewOwner {\n    var state: ReviewOutcome = .idle\n    var refreshCallback: (() -&gt; Void)?\n    let service: any ReviewServing\n\n    init(service: any ReviewServing) {\n        self.service = service\n        refreshCallback = { [weak self] in\n            guard let self else { return }\n            do {\n                self.state = .loaded(try self.service.names())\n            } catch {\n                self.state = .failed(\"Retry\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\nfunc summary(of state: ReviewOutcome) -&gt; String {\n    switch state {\n    case .idle: return \"Start\"\n    case let .loaded(names):\n        return names.isEmpty ? \"No matches\" : \"\\(names.count) providers\"\n    case let .failed(message): return message\n    }\n}\nfunc inspectReviewOwner() {\n    var owner: ReviewOwner? = ReviewOwner(\n        service: ReviewFake(result: .success([]))\n    )\n    let saved = owner?.refreshCallback\n    weak var probe = owner\n    saved?()\n    if let state = owner?.state {\n        print(summary(of: state)) // No matches\n    }\n    owner = nil\n    print(probe == nil) // true\n    saved?()            // missing owner: safely returns\n}\ninspectReviewOwner()\nlet offlineOwner = ReviewOwner(\n    service: ReviewFake(result: .failure(.offline))\n)\nofflineOwner.refreshCallback?()\nprint(summary(of: offlineOwner.state)) // Retry\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The controlled output is No matches, true, and Retry. The saved callback survives but its weak owner becomes nil, so the guard returns. The outcome enum preserves successful empty results and failure as different cases. Catching all errors as Retry is a scoped simplification; a production boundary may preserve typed causes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The initializer stores a newly constructed closure rather than accepting a closure parameter, so there is no parameter to mark escaping. The property's function type does not take an escaping attribute. If a future initializer accepts the stored callback from a caller, that plain closure parameter needs escaping and a fresh ownership review.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Complete your written audit by contrasting this pure program with the active UIKit coordinator. Source and test membership are verified through its Project.swift; the program itself does not verify navigation, task cancellation, localization, signing, or simulator behavior. Choose your next two retrieval tasks from the explanations you had to change, not from the ones that already felt comfortable.\u003c/p>"
      },
