      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write an incident artifact for a synthetic build whose confirmation screen sometimes terminates, using three intentionally different diagnostic cases: a trap, jetsam, and a slow request without termination.",
        "steps": [
          "Define eligible attempts, confirmed successes, expected cancellations, and app/build cohorts",
          "Choose the artifact required for each case and list the evidence needed before asserting a cause",
          "Draft an allowlisted event schema containing bounded state and failure code rather than request payloads",
          "Write containment actions with explicit limitations for installed clients",
          "Design one regression test and one post-release recovery check for the selected hypothesis"
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The artifact does not treat all three cases as ordinary crashes",
          "Symbol retention includes binary UUID matching",
          "The outcome metric has an explicit denominator",
          "Containment has an owner and an affected-client boundary",
          "Hypotheses, observations, and synthetic numbers remain distinguishable"
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>A worked artifact defines an eligible attempt as a user-confirmed submission accepted by the client while authenticated, deduplicated by an approved operation identity. Expected navigation cancellation before submission is excluded; timeout after submission remains unresolved until reconciliation. Success means the confirmed state was received and presented, not merely an HTTP success.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Case A has a symbolicated trap report for synthetic build 410. The matching dSYM UUID is verified; a mapper frame suggests a stale-index hypothesis. The proposed regression test replaces the result array before a delayed selection callback and verifies lookup by stable ID. That test supports the mechanism only after reproducing the failure in the candidate code. Case B has a jetsam report without an application backtrace: investigate repeated image retention and device memory pressure with allocation and ownership evidence. Case C stays alive but times out: follow its trace and reconcile server state before retrying.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The schema allows app/build, coarse OS cohort, operation state, bounded outcome code, and duration bucket. It excludes names, appointment details, tokens, and response bodies. Any operation identifier must receive its own access and retention review rather than being assumed anonymous.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Containment owner: release captain for rollout pause, feature owner for compatible flag disablement, service owner for reconciliation. Pausing does not downgrade build 410, and a remote flag cannot protect a path reached before flag retrieval. The recovery check compares confirmed-success rate and trap incidence within the same build/OS cohort after the fix, with enough attempts and an allowance for reporting delay. All incident data here is synthetic; your report must attach actual artifacts before claiming a diagnosed production defect.\u003c/p>"
      },
