      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Threat-model a synthetic appointment reminder that opens from a link, reads a session secret, displays cached metadata, and exports operational telemetry. Produce a five-row risk register and a verification plan.",
        "steps": [
          "Identify assets and separate external-link, service, storage, and telemetry trust boundaries",
          "Write one attacker capability and failure scenario for each boundary",
          "Choose a Keychain accessibility policy and document the foreground/background and migration tradeoff",
          "Draft an allowlisted telemetry schema and a safe account-switch cleanup contract",
          "Inspect the active query attributes and privacy-manifest packaging, recording questions rather than inventing vulnerabilities"
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Every risk has a concrete mitigation, owner, and verification",
          "The client is not treated as the service's authorization authority",
          "Token, appointment details, and arbitrary response bodies are excluded from proposed telemetry",
          "Keychain not-found and access failure remain distinct",
          "Declarations are separated from actual archive and runtime validation"
        ],
        "solution": "\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Risk\u003c/th>\u003cth>Accountable owner (proposed role)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mitigation and evidence\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Crafted link selects an unauthorized reminder\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Route feature lead with service authorization lead\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parse bounded route; service checks ownership; test foreign synthetic ID\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token appears in diagnostics\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Telemetry pipeline lead with privacy reviewer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Allowlist outcome fields; inspect synthetic exported event for secret absence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Secret unavailable while locked is mistaken for deletion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Credential platform lead\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Preserve status taxonomy; test locked-access behavior without clearing the item\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Previous account's cached reminder remains visible\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Account/session feature lead\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Scope cache by session and clear UI/cache on account transition; test account A to B\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Dependency introduces new declared API use\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dependency and release artifact lead with privacy reviewer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Review dependency manifest and archived resources against current Apple requirements\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003cp>The worked policy chooses when-unlocked device-only storage only if the reminder needs credentials while foregrounded. It explicitly accepts reauthentication after migration to another device. If a background requirement emerges, revise the decision after examining available protection classes and the product's exposure tolerance; do not silently weaken a shared wrapper.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The event schema includes a bounded route category, outcome code, build, and duration bucket. It excludes the raw link, token, reminder text, and patient identity. Any needed correlation ID has a separate retention and access decision. Cleanup clears the visible model and account-scoped cache, performs narrowly scoped credential deletion when appropriate, and coordinates service revocation independently.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The repo review notes that Query.Attribute lacks an explicit accessibility case, while Apple's documented default is when-unlocked. It asks which consumers need background access rather than declaring a defect. The privacy file is referenced by the active Tuist manifest, but the final archive and dependency coverage remain unverified in this lab. This is a completed worked risk-register example, not an audit certification for the real application.\u003c/p>\u003cp>These are proposed accountable roles, not confirmed company maintainers or approvals. The route lead collects rejected-link fixtures and the service lead owns authorization evidence; telemetry owns exported captures; credential platform owns the status/accessibility matrix; account/session owns invalidation through the final commit; release owns archive contents. Each owner records an evidence location, review date, residual risk, and stop/recovery condition. A missing owner blocks acceptance of that row. Specialist review participates in the decision without leaving accountability as “the team.”\u003c/p>"
      },
