      "exercise": {
        "brief": "In a separate scratch UIKit target, make a long title and short badge row adapt to changing width and Dynamic Type; document its priority policy.",
        "steps": [
          "Embed makeDemoRow once with top and horizontal constraints, then place a second version inside a correctly constrained vertical scroll view.",
          "Explain the 300-point and 220-point synthetic width budgets before running.",
          "Exercise long text, right-to-left layout, and accessibility text categories; switch to a vertical alternative if the product's minimum width cannot fit the badge.",
          "Inspect actual constraints and ambiguity; capture the observed width, category, and conflict-log result.",
          "Compare the active BookingTextField height and placeholder contracts with the ordinary UILabel row."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The badge remains readable at the declared minimum supported width, or the documented alternative arrangement activates.",
          "The title wraps without clipping and all scroll content remains reachable at accessibility sizes.",
          "Constraints are installed once and there are no unexplained unsatisfiable-constraint logs.",
          "The report distinguishes synthetic arithmetic, source evidence, and actual scratch observations."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The lower title compression priority makes it the initial narrowing participant. Multiline wrapping then turns narrower width into greater height. Higher badge hugging also discourages allocating surplus width to the badge. The external row width must be fixed by its parent for that prediction to be meaningful. A suitable scratch installation, after obtaining the row, is:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">row.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false\nview.addSubview(row)\nlet guide = view.safeAreaLayoutGuide\nNSLayoutConstraint.activate([\n    row.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: guide.topAnchor, constant: 16),\n    row.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: guide.leadingAnchor, constant: 16),\n    row.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: guide.trailingAnchor, constant: -16)\n])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This is a fragment inside a UIViewController method, not another standalone program. If the final content can exceed the viewport, use a scroll view rather than a required bottom equality that crushes the title. The arithmetic predicts a 32-point shortage at 220 points before wrapping; it is not a pixel-perfect prediction for a real font. The actual measurement must account for font metrics, text, and the layout's constraints.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A custom-height field also needs invalidation when its inputs change, as the repository example shows. Do not infer that a larger frame proves correct placeholder drawing. Record the largest text fixture and a keyboard-visible screenshot if appropriate. An unexecuted scratch run remains pending learner work; no production controller or baseline is changed by this solution.\u003c/p>"
      },
