      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Extend the bounded loader from session 015 with a resource/cancellation/cache contract and a gated synthetic workload. Compare limits by evidence rather than choosing maximum parallelism.",
        "steps": [
          "Implement a positive-limit window, indexed results, and cancellation checks before replacement work.",
          "Use an actor-backed fake to record active operations and controllable completion gates.",
          "Specify byte limits, partial-failure policy, service scope, and per-waiter versus shared cancellation.",
          "Add a fake condition matrix for low power, thermal severity and memory pressure; define admission, stopping, fallback and bounded recovery separately.",
          "Prepare limits 1, 2, and 4 as experiment conditions, recording throughput, p95, peak bytes, transfers, and pending energy fields."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Peak active calls never exceed the chosen limit, even when completions occur out of order.",
          "An empty input returns empty output and invalid limits fail explicitly.",
          "Cancellation/failure is never converted to successful partial output.",
          "Logout invalidation prevents old cache commits and an independent UI generation prevents stale publication.",
          "Optional work reacts to fake pressure changes without a restart storm or a false assumption that a warning always precedes termination.",
          "Energy fields require actual device/tool observations; request count alone is not labeled battery savings."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The window below is standalone Swift for Sendable string snapshots. It bounds created children but not the final output size, request rate, or each payload's bytes. Give those separate contracts in the harness.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">enum WindowError: Error { case invalidLimit, incomplete }\n\nfunc windowed(\n    _ ids: [String], limit: Int,\n    load: @escaping @Sendable (String) async throws -&gt; String\n) async throws -&gt; [String] {\n    guard limit &gt; 0 else { throw WindowError.invalidLimit }\n    try Task.checkCancellation()\n    return try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: (Int, String).self) { group in\n        var output = [String?](repeating: nil, count: ids.count)\n        var next = 0\n        for _ in 0..&lt;min(limit, ids.count) {\n            let index = next\n            let id = ids[index]\n            next += 1\n            group.addTask {\n                try Task.checkCancellation()\n                return (index, try await load(id))\n            }\n        }\n        while let (index, value) = try await group.next() {\n            try Task.checkCancellation()\n            output[index] = value\n            if next &lt; ids.count {\n                let index = next\n                let id = ids[index]\n                next += 1\n                group.addTask {\n                    try Task.checkCancellation()\n                    return (index, try await load(id))\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        try Task.checkCancellation()\n        return try output.map { value in\n            guard let value else { throw WindowError.incomplete }\n            return value\n        }\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>\u003ccode>@escaping\u003c/code> allows the loader to be retained by child closures; \u003ccode>@Sendable\u003c/code> constrains captures. Only the parent writes optional output slots. One completion frees one position in the active window before a replacement is added. The final map refuses a missing result instead of using compactMap to silently publish fewer items.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use gates to complete the third input first and prove input ordering. Cancel the outer task with children waiting, release/cancel fake gates, and verify no replacement or UI commit occurs after the relevant checks. A fake must cooperate or the structured scope will wait for it. Keep a service-owned generation for cache commits and a feature-owned generation for visible publication. Compare fixed workloads at each limit on the same device; do not infer an optimal number or energy improvement from this code alone.\u003c/p>"
      },
