      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Debug or paper-trace CourseCounter, then create an interoperability diagnostic note for the UIKit target/action example.",
        "steps": [
          "Predict count before and after one recordTap call and identify whether label is stored or computed.",
          "In a scratch host program, optionally set a breakpoint on recordTap and inspect stop reason, frame, count, and loaded image; otherwise mark the trace as predicted.",
          "Compare frame-variable inspection with deliberate evaluation of label, explaining side-effect boundaries.",
          "Read the active bridging-header and gesture-recognizer anchors; map header direction, @objc, #selector, stored closure, and owner lifetime.",
          "Classify a nil imported object, unrecognized selector, missing generated header, and mismatched release symbols."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "The counter prediction is zero then one, and computed-label evaluation is explained.",
          "Executed observations include artifact/configuration/stop scope; paper traces remain labeled predictions.",
          "No debugger command prints an account, token, entire request, environment, or signing configuration.",
          "The UIKit example is identified as target-dependent, with a retained callback owner and an isolation contract.",
          "Each failure is assigned an appropriate next check rather than a universal cache or signing fix."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The counter starts at zero and increments to one. The computed label then returns Taps: 1. Set a source breakpoint inside recordTap using the scratch file's actual line, inspect the selected frame, and compare count before and after stepping over the increment. A call to label runs its getter; a call to recordTap through expression would deliberately change the process and is unnecessary for this observation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the UIKit example, an owner must retain CourseTapTarget. Configure removes its external argument label, #selector(didTap(_:)) identifies a one-argument Objective-C method, and @objc exposes that method. MainActor requires appropriate UI access, while Objective-C exposure alone supplies no automatic thread hop. The active gesture source additionally stores a closure, whose captures need an independent ownership review.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A nil imported result suggests checking nullability and optional handling. An unrecognized selector suggests callback signature and Objective-C exposure. A missing generated Swift header suggests target/module identity and build membership. Mismatched release symbols require the actual binary's UUID-matching dSYMs. Host counter results do not validate UIKit delivery or a production crash; keep those observations pending unless executed in their proper targets.\u003c/p>"
      },
