      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Independently add CoursePolicy to your scratch CourseResources graph, verify gated schema acceptance, and submit a rollout/reconstruction rehearsal.",
        "steps": [
          "Record or predict the baseline resource tests and version-1-only acceptance contract.",
          "Create a public CoursePolicy library product, source target, and test target; add only the local package/product edge to CourseResources.",
          "Write decision fixtures for versions -1, 0, 1, 2, and 3 under both opt-in states before comparing the implementation.",
          "Add consumer validation, preserve loader failures, and test synthetic schema-1/schema-2 resources with the independent condition absent and present.",
          "Introduce an overly broad opt-in bug, predict its failing case, restore the policy, and record actual or predicted verification.",
          "Write separate rollback paths for removing the opt-in and removing the dependency, plus a tabletop distributed-client recovery plan."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Version 1 accepts in both states; version 2 accepts only when explicitly opted in; invalid/future inputs reject.",
          "The public policy is usable through an ordinary cross-module import, and package declaration, target product edge, and source import are all present.",
          "Resource ownership stays with CourseResources and missing/decode/unsupported errors remain distinguishable.",
          "DEBUG, PROD, COURSE_NEXT_SCHEMA, and runtime flags are not conflated.",
          "The evidence ledger names executed/predicted/pending checks and observed test discovery counts.",
          "The reconstruction plan restores consumer code, graph inputs, conditions, and tests together; it promises no downgrade of installed store clients."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The worked CoursePolicy manifest exposes its public source through a library product and gives tests their own target. Put the policy implementation from the lesson in Sources/CoursePolicy. The tests use an ordinary import, proving the public access boundary rather than relying on @testable.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">// swift-tools-version: 5.9\nimport PackageDescription\nlet package = Package(\n    name: \"CoursePolicy\",\n    products: [\n        .library(name: \"CoursePolicy\", targets: [\"CoursePolicy\"])\n    ],\n    targets: [\n        .target(name: \"CoursePolicy\"),\n        .testTarget(name: \"CoursePolicyTests\", dependencies: [\"CoursePolicy\"])\n    ]\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">import XCTest\nimport CoursePolicy\n\nfinal class PolicyContractTests: XCTestCase {\n    func testSupportedAndUnknownVersions() {\n        let cases: [(Int, Bool, SchemaDecision)] = [\n            (-1, false, .reject), (-1, true, .reject),\n            (0, false, .reject), (0, true, .reject),\n            (1, false, .accept), (1, true, .accept),\n            (2, false, .reject), (2, true, .accept),\n            (3, false, .reject), (3, true, .reject)\n        ]\n        for (version, allowNext, expected) in cases {\n            XCTAssertEqual(\n                schemaDecision(for: version, allowNext: allowNext),\n                expected,\n                \"version=\\(version), opt-in=\\(allowNext)\"\n            )\n        }\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This is one discovered XCTest method with ten fixture rows. The consumer declaration and validator above complete the dependency seam. To opt in locally, add \u003ccode>swiftSettings: [.define(\"COURSE_NEXT_SCHEMA\")]\u003c/code> to the scratch CourseResources production target, after its resource field; removing that setting restores the absent-condition branch. This is an independent lab condition, not an instruction to edit Debug or production settings in the client. Keep the consumer test target.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The schema-1 fixture passes validation in both builds. A schema-2 fixture rejects without the condition and accepts with it. The missing-name path still throws ResourceFailure.missing, and corrupt JSON still fails decoding. For a loader-independent error assertion, call \u003ccode>validateSchema(2, allowNext: false)\u003c/code> and check the unsupportedSchema associated value. The deliberate accept-everything mutation fails invalid/future opt-in rows.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reconstruction first removes the scratch define and reruns baseline behavior. Complete dependency removal also restores pre-change consumer code and removes the local package/product/import together. A real remote rollback would restore the relevant pins and artifact inputs. In the tabletop release note, compiler-condition changes require a new build, runtime containment has explicit limits, and installed App Store clients require a corrective release. All actual client, device, archive, distribution, licensing, and security checks remain pending.\u003c/p>"
      },
