      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Create a synthetic refresh lease adapter and a verification ledger. Simulate success, expiration, late success, and logout; do not register real pushes or mutate client configuration.",
        "steps": [
          "Use WorkFinishGate with a fake completion recorder and a retained Task handle.",
          "Give the store an account epoch and an allowed-to-commit flag; serialize both validation and replacement.",
          "Drive expiration before releasing a gated fake response, then release it anyway.",
          "Repeat with normal success, ordinary failure, duplicate finish calls, and A-to-B-to-A account changes.",
          "Write an APNs artifact checklist and mark physical-device delivery as pending."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Exactly one terminal completion is recorded per fake lease.",
          "Expiration cancels work and prevents a later response from replacing the snapshot.",
          "Changing account ownership blocks old writes even if the account text becomes A again.",
          "The ledger distinguishes source entitlements from signed archive evidence.",
          "No timing, APNs delivery, or background-transfer verification is claimed from the fake."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>A worked adapter has one owner for terminal state and one owner for durable commit. For the scratch experiment keep both on MainActor so no await separates the ownership check from the replacement. Use session 020's gated-fake pattern to control ordering. The following value is only a synthetic commit boundary, not a production database:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">@MainActor\nfinal class RefreshCommit {\n    private var epoch = 0\n    private var accepting = true\n    private(set) var snapshot: [String] = []\n\n    func ticket() -&gt; Int { epoch }\n\n    func close() {\n        accepting = false\n        epoch += 1\n    }\n\n    func startNewOwner() {\n        epoch += 1\n        accepting = true\n        snapshot = []\n    }\n\n    func commit(_ values: [String], ticket: Int) -&gt; Bool {\n        guard accepting, ticket == epoch else { return false }\n        snapshot = values\n        return true\n    }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Capture \u003ccode>ticket()\u003c/code> before awaiting the fake remote. On expiration call \u003ccode>close()\u003c/code>, cancel the retained task, and finish the gate as expired. When the fake is released, check Task cancellation, then call commit with the original ticket; it must fail even if the fake ignored cancellation. Normal success commits first and reports success only after that succeeds. Ordinary failure preserves the previous snapshot and reports failure. A second terminal call has no effect.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003ccode>private(set)\u003c/code> lets the harness inspect the snapshot but keeps mutation inside the owner. Incrementing epoch invalidates all prior tickets. Starting another owner increments it again, so textual account equality cannot restore validity. The methods are synchronous; a disk adapter would need to perform the same validation in its actual transaction owner, not validate here and later await an unguarded file write.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The worked ledger records the fake's completion sequence, snapshot before and after each case, and pending device rows. The APNs row requests the installed bundle ID, signed aps environment, compatible profile/export path, provider endpoint and topic, current token binding, payload push type, and observed response. A foreground banner screenshot answers only presentation. No exact scheduler cadence or clinical reminder reliability follows from this exercise.\u003c/p>"
      },
