      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Write a labeled distribution helper returning an optional quotient/remainder tuple and a caption helper with a default suffix.",
        "steps": [
          "Declare distribute(_ total: Int, among count: Int) with a named optional tuple result.",
          "Reject count zero or negative using guard.",
          "Call it with seven/two and seven/zero, preserving nil.",
          "Declare caption(for name: String, suffix: String = \"ready\") and call both forms.",
          "Explain each external label and each return path."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Seven/two returns whole three and remainder one.",
          "Invalid divisors return nil before arithmetic.",
          "The caption default and explicit suffix produce distinct expected strings.",
          "The solution uses no casts, force unwraps or client dependencies."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Here is a complete standalone answer. Tuple extraction stays inside optional binding, and the caption returns data so the caller chooses when to print.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">func distribute(_ total: Int, among count: Int) -&gt; (whole: Int, remainder: Int)? {\n    guard count &gt; 0 else { return nil }\n    return (total / count, total % count)\n}\nfunc caption(for name: String, suffix: String = \"ready\") -&gt; String {\n    return \"\\(name): \\(suffix)\"\n}\nif let parts = distribute(7, among: 2) {\n    print(parts.whole)\n    print(parts.remainder)\n}\nprint(distribute(7, among: 0) == nil)\nprint(caption(for: \"Rae\"))\nprint(caption(for: \"Rae\", suffix: \"waiting\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Expected lines are 3, 1, true, Rae: ready and Rae: waiting. Tuple labels come from the declared return contract even though the return expression uses positional construction. The local name count is supplied under external among; total has no external label. Nil means this helper could not produce its result for the stated divisor, not that either component equals zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To test transfer, change the valid input to eight among three and predict two and two. Then state an additional domain rule, such as forbidding negative totals, and decide where to validate it. Do not assume positive divisor validation already covers that separate product requirement. Your own helper, predictions and observed run establish exercise evidence. These small synchronous operations do not establish service effects, actor isolation, or UI formatting correctness.\u003c/p>"
      },
