        "solution": "\u003cp>Run this complete program separately from earlier snippets. The guards use current collection index domains rather than hard-coded assumptions.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">let original = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Rae\"]\nvar edited = original\nedited[1] = \"Kai\"\nedited.append(\"Sam\")\nprint(original)\nprint(edited)\nvar empty: [String] = []\nprint(empty.first == nil)\nprint(empty.popLast() == nil)\nlet slice = original[1..&lt;3]\nlet rebuilt = Array(slice)\nprint(slice.startIndex)\nprint(rebuilt.startIndex)\nif slice.indices.contains(1) {\n    print(slice[1])\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The two array prints differ only in the edited value. Both empty checks print true. Start indices print one and zero, and the final line is Lin. Contains is evaluated against the slice’s own indices, so it does not mistakenly authorize index zero. The original let prevents mutating its array, while edited’s var permits replacement and append.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a transfer fixture, slice 2..\u003c3 and predict a start index of two and a single Rae element. If you need to keep that slice long-term, explain whether preserving source coordinates or creating an independent array container better serves the consumer. Do not claim a measured memory improvement from this small run. Record the actual or explicitly predicted output as your learner evidence, separate from the author’s worked answer.\u003c/p>"
