        "solution": "\u003cp>This complete standalone implementation uses only constructs already introduced. Insert’s named tuple member provides the new-member decision without a second membership lookup.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">let labels = [\"general\", \"dental\", \"general\", \"vision\"]\nvar counts: [String: Int] = [:]\nvar seen: Set&lt;String&gt; = []\nvar ordered: [String] = []\nfor label in labels {\n    counts[label, default: 0] += 1\n    if seen.insert(label).inserted {\n        ordered.append(label)\n    }\n}\nfor key in counts.keys.sorted() {\n    if let value = counts[key] { print(\"\\(key): \\(value)\") }\n}\nprint(ordered)\ncounts[\"empty\"] = 0\nprint(counts[\"absent\"] == nil)\nprint(counts[\"empty\"] == 0)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The histogram prints dental one, general two and vision one in sorted-key order. Ordered contains general, dental and vision in their first appearance order, independent of set traversal. Both final checks print true, while the dictionary states they test differ: absent has no association; empty has a present zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To test transfer, begin the input with vision and confirm that the unique output begins with vision while the sorted histogram still begins with dental. That checks the two ordering contracts independently. If duplicate labels should be rejected rather than counted, state a different rule before changing implementation. The present exercise validates synthetic value behavior and does not establish production identifier correctness or benchmark hashing performance.\u003c/p>"
