        "acceptance": [
          "Success consumes the value and failure chooses a documented recovery.",
          "A pre-cancelled producer never executes its body, and the consumer handles no result.",
          "Ordering uses addDependency rather than FIFO or QoS.",
          "Every manual completion signal is balanced and waits are finite/off UI executors."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Keep one locked optional Result for the producer output and a separate event log. Success stores .success(17), failure stores .failure(.fixture), and cancellation before start leaves nil. The consumer's switch has three branches: apply a successful value, record the supported recovery for a failure, or record stopped prerequisite for nil. Use an immutable case value captured by the producer rather than mutating a global scenario between enqueues.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Add consumer.addDependency(producer) before either enqueue. That makes the consumer's readiness depend on finished state; the array's submission order is deliberately opposite so the fixture cannot accidentally rely on FIFO. Put the test's finite completion signal on the consumer and check the event log after that signal. A completion callback signals the host only; it is not the producer's result transport. If the cancellation fixture cancelled the consumer too, its body should not be expected to record a business event; inspect finished and side-effect absence instead.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The shipped baseline already verifies failure recovery and a separately pre-cancelled BlockOperation. The learner's three-way fixture remains an independent exercise. A source cancellation test should wait for its cancellation handler before reclaiming a descriptor, while a group timeout should report unfinished work rather than cancelling by implication. QoS stays a workload hint throughout. The main-thread-to-worker-to-main wait graph contains a cycle; explain the remedy as asynchronous result delivery, without manufacturing deadlock evidence.\u003c/p>"
