        "solution": "\u003cp>Add a string NSAttributeDescription named note, set isOptional to true, and append it to both model definitions. Populate only B's note in the old-store seed; leaving A nil is an intentional historical state. In rows, read value(forKey:) inside performAndWait and render nil as a fixed synthetic sentinel distinct from an empty string. Compare A and B projections explicitly. A successful default for origin must not accidentally replace the unrelated optional note. This forces the exercise to preserve absence as well as text.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The accepted path still uses source metadata, inferred mapping, a distinct destination, and post-migration validation. For the rejected path, supply a deliberately wrong expected projection to an acceptance function after migration. That function throws a preservation error and does not invoke any replacement operation. Keep the original source URL untouched and refetch its old rows. Do not modify the old bytes to make the candidate assertion pass. The test distinguishes a framework-compatible candidate from one your product is prepared to publish.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Write the cutover answer as ordered ownership: stop scratch writers, close/reset contexts, validate the candidate and account-scoped invariants, perform a supported replacement only under the designated owner, reopen with the destination schema, then publish a ready state. If replacement or reopen fails, preserve diagnostics and use the retained old-store recovery procedure rather than silently constructing a new store. Define when retained sources are deleted and how their protection matches current data. The lab supplies preservation evidence but leaves process-death-at-cutover and device protection for a dedicated target test.\u003c/p>\u003cp>SwiftData uses the same optional-value fixture idea, but its schema declaration and migration plan remain separate source. Its macro helper may need the documented compiler flag inside this workspace sandbox. Record any compile or runtime failure honestly; a Core Data pass cannot substitute for a SwiftData pass. No exercise changes course progress or the client's persistent stores.\u003c/p>"
