      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Add a bounded conflict-resolution operation and prove that a delete followed by an explicit recreate remains account-scoped after restart.",
        "steps": [
          "Run HostChecks and both RestartProbe modes in fresh isolated directories.",
          "Seed an accepted create, then a delete with the correct base revision, and reload before replay.",
          "Create a deliberately stale edit and assert the FIFO pauses without acknowledging later work.",
          "Implement or independently reproduce the bounded resolveConflict actor command: require one pending command for this record and replace the conflicting entry in its original queue slot with a fresh-ID request. Reject dependent edits unless an explicit atomic reconciliation policy handles them.",
          "Test A-to-B-to-A between sending and acknowledgment; record which effect already happened remotely and which local commit was rejected."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Ordinary process restart reloads pending intent and stable operation identity.",
          "A lost response plus replay creates one synthetic server effect, including after receipt reload.",
          "Delete intent outlives the removed local row; stale updates cannot resurrect it automatically.",
          "Rebase has a fresh ID, preserves its queue position and commits queue/local state together; multiple pending edits to its record are rejected without changing bytes, and unrelated accounts remain unchanged.",
          "Disk/cancellation/conflict failures remain distinct and no device/background/production behavior is claimed."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>The supplied resolveConflict reference lives inside DurableOutbox. Validate cancellation and the account ticket, locate a command explicitly marked conflict, and require exactly one pending command for its record. Copy the journal and replace the entry at its original index with a fresh-ID request and the supplied current base revision. Update the copied optimistic record, then call commit once. Do not remove and append: with two queued edits to the same record, a later edit could execute first, advance the server revision, and make the replacement conflict again. This bounded API throws dependentCommands for that case without changing bytes or actor memory. A broader implementation must reconcile every dependent intent and precondition in one documented atomic operation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>HostChecks.rebaseChecks demonstrates both schedules. One conflicted edit is replaced in its original slot, its fresh ID produces one additional server effect, and its original receipt remains conflict. In the two-command fixture, the head conflicts while the later edit targets the current revision. Resolution is rejected, the complete queue and its file bytes stay unchanged, and FIFO replay produces no later effect. An injected failed write also preserves the original conflict. These checks establish the bounded local policy; a supplied current revision can still become stale before a real server receives the replacement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the delete/recreate answer, the original create uses revision zero, the accepted delete uses revision one, and an explicit recreate uses revision two because the synthetic tombstone still carries the newer revision. The recreate is a new user intent with a fresh operation ID. Replay the queued delete before recreating, or define the dependency as a single ordered lane. Assert the server value is nil after delete acknowledgment and only becomes the chosen new title after the new command is accepted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the manual acknowledgment seam for the account schedule: capture A's ticket, send the command to the synthetic server, activate B, activate A again, and call record with the old ticket. staleAccount is the expected local result. Fetch B through its own current activation and verify its records remain unrelated. Then reactivate A, replay its retained pending command, and resolve the existing server receipt without another effect. This proves ownership at the local commit boundary rather than pretending the first service effect disappeared.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finally, preserve the original before-image file for an injected RejectingDisk test. Both the bytes and a same-actor read must still reflect the acknowledged state after failure. Keep the ordinary restart proof and these injected failure proofs in separate evidence rows. A crash during rename, actual disk exhaustion, scheduler grants and receipt-retention negotiation remain further verification, with no automatic learner completion.\u003c/p>"
      },
