      "exercise": {
        "brief": "Extend the mock flow with a denied refresh and an explicit recovery state, then draft a Keychain/background policy without performing real account operations.",
        "steps": [
          "Run the RFC S256 vector and existing login-refresh-logout/vault checks.",
          "Add a provider test seam that denies one refresh and verify no new credentials are published.",
          "Hold a synthetic exchange result, start another attempt or logout, then attempt the old install.",
          "Test locked save and failed deletion; preserve the cleanup obligation and prevent stale installation.",
          "Write the production adapter policy for accessibility, status mapping, refresh coalescing and durable sign-out; identify the passkey/server-verification seam."
        ],
        "acceptance": [
          "Wrong or duplicate state, code reuse, and old epochs never publish credentials.",
          "A failed vault write cannot be described as a durable authenticated session.",
          "Logout closes local authorization even when deletion fails, and the remaining cleanup is explicit.",
          "The policy separates synthetic execution from browser, Keychain, passkey and real-provider evidence."
        ],
        "solution": "\u003cp>Add a failNextRefresh property to MockProvider and a method used only by tests to enable it. In refresh, inspect the seam before consuming the old token, clear the seam, and throw a typed synthetic denial. This fixture choice makes a pre-rotation failure deterministic; it must not be generalized to every provider rejection. Capture current credentials, attempt refresh, assert the expected failure and unchanged current view, then retry according to your explicitly selected policy. Add a separate post-rotation/lost-response fixture if investigating ambiguous token rotation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the manual install seam to order stale outcomes without sleeps. Begin attempt A, obtain its provider result, begin B or logout, then call install with A's epoch. Expect stale and inspect the current state. In the deletion-denied schedule, current must be nil while cleanupPending remains true. Clear the fake denial, call retryCleanup, and assert the obligation ends. This demonstrates local state recovery; it deliberately does not persist the marker or read surviving Keychain material after restart.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The adapter policy chooses foreground-only device-only accessibility for this synthetic editor and accepts reauthentication after restoration to another device. If background credentials become a requirement, justify the after-first-unlock alternative and verify before-first-unlock behavior on a signed device. Treat not-found as absence, interaction restrictions as unavailable, duplicate add as bounded update/requery, and entitlement/parameter failures as configuration. Keep the query identity narrow on every operation and retain no real secrets in learner artifacts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Finally, describe one shared refresh operation with waiter cancellation and account invalidation as separate inputs, and a durable sign-out marker consulted before credential reload. For passkeys, place server-verified challenge results upstream of session installation; keep browser, relying-party association and signature verification outside the fake provider's proof. The finished exercise is a reviewable mock extension and recovery policy, not a production authentication certification.\u003c/p>"
      },
