        "prompt": "Explain why a service consumer loses retry behavior with try?, and identify the separate contract change needed when it starts storing its success callback.",
        "scenario": "A synchronous synthetic appointment consumer evaluates (try? service.appointments()) ?? [] and invokes onSuccess. A later revision saves onSuccess in a handler for invocation after the function returns.",
        "androidBridge": "Kotlin runCatching followed by getOrDefault(emptyList()) can erase failures like Swift try? followed by ?? []. Kotlin callbacks have no direct @escaping counterpart: Swift uses the annotation to permit a plain closure parameter to outlive its call, without specifying a thread or dispatcher.",
        "rubric": [
          "Identifies offline and empty success as the outcomes being merged.",
          "Replaces fallback with explicit propagation or do/catch and distinct callbacks.",
          "Requires @escaping when the plain callback parameter is stored.",
          "Separates escaping from asynchronous execution, capture ownership, and guaranteed invocation."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>\u003ccode>try?\u003c/code> discards the thrown error and returns nil for failure. The following empty-array fallback makes offline indistinguishable from a successful response containing no appointments. The compiler accepts this, but the consumer loses the information needed to offer retry. Use \u003ccode>do/catch\u003c/code> with separate success and failure paths, or propagate the error to an owner that can map it deliberately. Successful empty data must still take the success path.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The original synchronous callbacks need no escaping annotation because they run before the consumer returns. Once the plain callback parameter is stored, mark that parameter \u003ccode>@escaping\u003c/code> and review the retaining handler and captured objects. Escaping permits a longer lifetime; it establishes neither background execution nor weak capture. Define how often completion can occur and when the handler releases the callback. Test nonempty success, empty success, and offline independently, including cleanup when delayed work is abandoned.\u003c/p>",
