        "prompt": "Audit a nested closure after weak self is promoted, and choose a lifetime policy for queued work that may outlive a dismissed screen.",
        "scenario": "Screen owns a queue of callbacks. An outer [weak self] callback performs guard let self, then stores an inner closure on that queue which uses self. The inner work can remain queued indefinitely.",
        "androidBridge": "Kotlin coroutine or callback captures can also retain a screen beyond its intended lifecycle. Lifecycle cancellation is a separate policy in both systems; Swift's outer weak capture does not automatically apply to a nested closure, and ARC cycles differ from JVM reachability collection.",
        "rubric": [
          "Labels the inner closure's capture of the promoted self as strong.",
          "Identifies the possible Screen to queue to closure to Screen cycle.",
          "Distinguishes bounded operation retention from indefinite retention.",
          "Selects weak screen updates or a dedicated operation owner according to required behavior."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The outer weak capture changes only that closure's edge. After \u003ccode>guard let self\u003c/code>, the body has a strong local reference. An inner stored closure using that binding can capture it strongly, creating screen-to-queue-to-closure-to-screen ownership. The outer annotation does not propagate a weak policy into nested work.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Decide whether the queued operation belongs to the screen. If its only purpose is updating a visible screen, give the inner callback its own weak capture and define removal or cancellation when the flow ends. If a save must finish after dismissal, a dedicated operation owner can retain the service and data it needs, while treating screen updates separately. Strong retention until a reliable completion and release path is bounded ownership; work queued indefinitely lacks that assurance. Inspect completion, cancellation, and queue clearing, then use a controlled pending callback to test release. Replacing weak with unowned would substitute a lifetime assumption and possible trap for an ownership policy.\u003c/p>",
