        "prompt": "Implement the RFC's pure slot mapper so it retains complete first-occurrence values in input order, then specify fixtures that catch a wrong duplicate-title policy.",
        "scenario": "SyntheticSlot has id: String and title: String. Input is a/First, b/Second, a/Replacement. Expected output is a/First and b/Second; IDs are case-sensitive, input is unchanged, and the mapper has no UI isolation requirement.",
        "androidBridge": "Kotlin filtering with a mutable membership set expresses the same algorithmic boundary. Swift structs and arrays have value semantics, while Set iteration does not promise input order; a protocol adapter is a replacement seam rather than a requirement for a particular Android or iOS DI framework.",
        "rubric": [
          "Uses an ID membership set while traversing the original input.",
          "Preserves the first complete slot rather than reconstructing only IDs.",
          "Checks conflicting titles, empty, all-duplicate, distinct, and case-sensitive fixtures.",
          "Keeps the mapper pure and distinguishes algorithmic reasoning from measured performance."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Use the set only for membership and append the original first value during input traversal. This keeps order and retains its title:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode class=\"language-swift\">struct SyntheticSlot: Equatable {\n    let id: String\n    let title: String\n}\nfunc mapSlots(_ input: [SyntheticSlot]) -&gt; [SyntheticSlot] {\n    var seen = Set&lt;String&gt;()\n    return input.filter { seen.insert($0.id).inserted }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The input fixture returns a/First followed by b/Second. Comparing only IDs would miss accidentally retaining Replacement. Assert complete output values, plus empty input, all duplicates, all distinct, and IDs differing only in case. The contract deliberately introduces no case folding. Keep this pure implementation behind the bounded mapping seam so the old implementation can be compared using synthetic fixtures without duplicating side effects. A pure mapper needs no MainActor annotation merely because another module protocol has one. Set membership adds storage and changes expected work; representative CPU, memory, and screen measurements are still required before claiming an optimization outcome.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "Why is shadow comparison safe for this mapper but unsafe for booking submission?",
          "Which consumer test protects selection when row order changes?"
        ],
        "pitfalls": [
          "Deduplicating IDs and rebuilding slots with the last title.",
          "Returning set iteration order or adding case folding without changing the contract."
        ]
