        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Give the page owner a query generation, next cursor, matching in-flight cursor, ordered IDs, and end state. Repeated threshold callbacks should start work only when that generation and cursor have no active request. Capture both before suspension. On success or failure, verify they still belong to the current query before changing rows, error state, or the in-flight marker. An old completion must not unlock a newer request. Serialize cursor-based loads or define explicit buffering if pages may finish out of order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Preserve useful rows on a later-page failure and retry the failed cursor under the same guard. Define duplicate-ID merging and repeated-cursor behavior. Prefetch is a separate resource-interest mechanism: translate callback positions to current IDs, and ensure visible configuration can load missing content without any prefetch callback. Canceling one speculative interest should not cancel a shared image still needed by a visible consumer. Use controlled start signals and completion gates to release B before A, then exercise A's success and error paths.\u003c/p>",
