        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Stored count owns state. A computed caption runs its getter and reflects the value read at that access unless the getter implements another contract. A lazy caption initializes stored state on its first access and retains that snapshot afterward; changing count does not automatically invalidate it. Lazy must be var, and first access on a struct can require a mutable receiver.\u003c/p>\u003cp>WillSet/didSet observe assignments with proposed/previous values and can run for same-value assignments. Ordinary direct initial assignments establishing the type’s own stored properties do not invoke them, so observers are not a substitute for validating construction. A failable init? returns nil for invalid input rather than a partially usable quantity. Bind that optional before use. If the reason matters, a throwing initializer can preserve it instead. Visibility and isolation remain separate: none of these property spellings guarantees thread-safe first access, transaction rollback or a public construction API.\u003c/p>",
