        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Two variables assigned the same Counter reference identify one instance. A let binding prevents replacing that reference, not changing the instance’s var value, so mutation through one alias is visible through the other. Use === to compare class identity. Equal property values in two separately constructed Counters do not make their identities the same; == needs an equality contract and asks a different question.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A Record-typed reference holding TaggedRecord still has TaggedRecord as its dynamic type. Calling the overridable caption selects the subclass implementation. Upcasting exposes the superclass interface without constructing a new base object or discarding tag storage. Downcasting with as? can conditionally reveal subclass capabilities while retaining identity. Prefer a declared polymorphic operation when the consumer only needs behavior, and inspect ownership/isolation if shared mutation is intentional. These predictions establish reference and dispatch semantics, not thread safety or UI lifecycle.\u003c/p>",
