        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Once reached, each defer registers work for the exit of its enclosing scope. A thrown guard error leaves the inner scope first, running its cleanup, then leaves the function and runs outer cleanup, then reaches the caller’s catch. Multiple defers in one scope run in reverse registration order. A defer on an unreachable path is never registered; it does not catch or erase the error.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Try marks the throwing call. Catch handles the matching error payload, allowing the caller to retain the invalid input and explain recovery. Try? would convert the thrown reason to nil, and a zero fallback could then misrepresent failure as accepted data. Try! instead traps on an error and is not a user-facing repair. Force unwraps, invalid subscripts and failed preconditions are also not ordinary thrown errors recovered by this catch. Test normal and thrown exits while labeling print cleanup as scope evidence rather than proof of real resource release.\u003c/p>",
