        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A generic parameter names a type relationship: identity’s T input returns T without a cast, and Source’s associated Element can remain visible through a constrained generic. Uppercase Any instead accepts unrestricted values with little declared capability. Lowercase any Protocol is an existential exposing that protocol boundary while hiding the concrete conformer, which may be a struct or class.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Result-position some Protocol hides one chosen underlying implementation type, so ordinary branches cannot return unrelated concrete conformers merely because both satisfy the protocol. Parameter-position some is generic shorthand; independent some parameters do not imply one shared type. A constrained existential can retain an associated fact such as Element == Int, so any does not always discard every relationship. Uppercase Self refers to the current/conforming type, while lowercase self is the current instance. Choose the contract from information the caller needs, then test that boundary before claiming optimization benefits.\u003c/p>",
