        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Use CADisplayLink when the component needs updates aligned with display activity. Read timestamp and targetTimestamp rather than assuming every display refresh lasts sixteen milliseconds. The device and system can change cadence; a preferred rate is a preference, not a budget guaranteed to the callback. If ordinary UIKit animation APIs express the behavior adequately, consider them before owning a custom loop.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Give one main-actor component explicit start and stop methods. Starting replaces any previous link. Stopping invalidates it and clears the reference at the agreed visibility boundary. A link retains its target, so relying solely on deinit can leave the owner alive. Common-mode registration may suit this visual feature, but still adds work during interaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Keep each callback to a small visual commit and compute heavy input elsewhere with a separate lifetime. Build and trace an independent target under representative scrolling, dismissal and refresh conditions. Record callback occupancy and actual deadline evidence. If it exceeds the intended cost, reduce or remove custom work and retest; a host mode check proves none of those device outcomes.\u003c/p>",
