        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The invariant is selecting one terminal outcome and transferring ownership of its waiter exactly once. A short synchronous lock fits racing callbacks because neither needs to suspend inside that transition. NSLock can support older deployment floors with a reviewed private wrapper. Mutex colocates protected state and requires the Synchronization toolchain plus supported OS availability, including iOS 18. OSAllocatedUnfairLock is available earlier on iOS 16 and provides stable allocated storage; a raw Swift os_unfair_lock value is not an equivalent safe substitution.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An actor is attractive if callers naturally use async isolation, but synchronous cancellation callbacks still need a safe handoff and an ownership policy. No thread-owned lock may span await. Whichever lock is chosen, store terminal state and remove the waiter atomically, release, then resume. A late callback sees terminal and does nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Name the adapter owner and the cancellation-before-registration path. Test both winning orders, late duplicate callbacks and cleanup after an error. If a legacy operation cannot stop immediately, retain its resources until completion while suppressing invalid publication. Passing a counter stress test alone does not prove this gate's protocol.\u003c/p>",
