        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Detached means unstructured work with different inherited context; it does not allocate a private thread or an unlimited blocking pool. A semaphore wait occupies the worker executing that task. If the children need workers from the same cooperative resource, enough waiting parents can consume progress capacity. The dependency pattern is wrong even if a lightly loaded machine happens to complete it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Directly await the child operation so the parent suspends instead of blocking. Prefer structured children where their lifetime belongs to the parent. For an unavoidable synchronous legacy call, use an owned bounded queue adapter and suspend the async caller through a continuation. Bound submission as well as executing operations; otherwise queued captures can grow.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Priority escalation is no substitute for those boundaries and promises no deadline or FIFO order. Wire cancellation to the underlying handle only if its API supports it, protect registration races, and resume exactly once. If the call cannot be interrupted, say that cancellation waits for completion and rejects the result afterward. Test semantic outcomes without deliberately exhausting workers or hanging a demonstration process.\u003c/p>",
