        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Create an admission owner rather than escalating all tasks. Define separate limits for active CPU jobs, active blocking reads, pending items and retained bytes. Reserve the next available capacity for visible demand or replace obsolete prefetches. Age pending work if starvation matters. A fixed worker count or task priority cannot implement these product decisions on its own.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Move CPU work through an explicit concurrent helper with chunk-level cancellation checks, retaining indexed results for deterministic presentation. Use an asynchronous API for supported transport and a bounded synchronous adapter only for genuinely legacy blocking reads. The owner cancels pending tokens and rejects stale generations. Once a noninterruptible read begins, retain its resources until it finishes, even if its result is no longer wanted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Compare representative foreground latency distributions, cancellation delay, backlog and memory under controlled workloads and device conditions. Consider fewer prefetches or eliminating redundant computation before adding workers. Roll out an admission change behind a bounded configuration with one responsible owner; revert or disable speculation if foreground regressions or stranded waiters appear. Host checksum checks establish mechanics, not production performance or energy gains.\u003c/p>",
