        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A newer compiler can compile several language modes. Swift 5 compatibility with complete concurrency checking can surface migration warnings, while Swift 6 makes race-safety violations language errors. The tools-version header governs manifest APIs and minimum tooling; it is not sufficient evidence of every target's language mode. Record the consuming target's flags rather than inferring them from Xcode's version.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The global counter lacks an isolation owner. Choose MainActor for UI-owned state, an actor for independently owned state, or an immutable constant if mutation is unnecessary. Merely lowering a historical checking setting is not a Swift 6 repair. Default MainActor inference and NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault are additional module settings that should be reviewed separately. Their behavior can change even when diagnostics improve.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The companion proves warning versus rejection with explicit compiler commands. It does not demonstrate a running race or a migrated client. Kotlin's compatibility settings and Android API requirements encourage the same input separation, but neither supplies identical checking rules. A defensible migration report names tested compiler/mode pairs and leaves target, runtime and adoption evidence pending.\u003c/p>",
