        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>A process can own shared services while each scene owns navigation and presentation. A UISceneSession carries persistent interaction identity; a connected UIScene and its windows/controllers are live UI objects. scene(_:willConnectTo:options:) can create or restore an interface for an existing session, so it is not a new-session test. Disconnection is also different from permanently discarding that session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inactive can describe a temporary interruption without entering background. Reactivation therefore need not include a background-to-foreground callback. A view's scenePhase reflects its containing scene, while an App-level phase aggregates scenes and can stay active while another window is inactive. Quiet the affected visual work without indiscriminately cancelling a shared account service used elsewhere.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Controller disappearance, view reconstruction, process launch, and durable draft lifetime are separate boundaries. SwiftUI view descriptions can change while identity-associated state survives. Preserve important drafts through normal durable commits: a suspended process may terminate without applicationWillTerminate. The familiar launch/foreground/active path is illustrative rather than universal. Use WindowGroup for scene roots and one delegate adaptor for needed process-level callbacks, then verify actual routes and transitions on a destination.\u003c/p>",
