        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>I would seed a V1 SQLite store with two accounts sharing an ID and different titles, save it on its context queue, and close its writer. That establishes historical persisted data rather than reconstructing current-model objects. V2 attaches the old canonical rename identity to displayTitle and gives origin a meaningful historical default. I would retain both model versions and either use supported automatic inference or explicitly obtain a mapping and migrate into a separate candidate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After opening that candidate with V2, I would compare complete account, ID, title and origin projections against expected values. Equal counts could hide lost text or cross-account replacement. I would also reopen the preserved source with V1 and inject unsupported-source and destination-failure cases.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A JSON decoder version test verifies another format, and an in-memory CRUD test skips SQLite compatibility. The evidence must name the actual store, toolchain and assertions. Candidate acceptance is separate from cutover, which needs quiesced contexts, sidecar-aware replacement and a compatible recovery path.\u003c/p>",
