        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The missing callbacks are a harness ownership error. OperationQueue may skip main for an operation cancelled before execution, so bookkeeping located only there never runs. Decrementing pending optimistically in the cancel button risks a second decrement if an executing operation later completes. I would choose one terminal-accounting owner and prove exactly-once completion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>ProfilingLab uses a lock-protected request flag instead of Operation.cancel for accepted attempts. Queued actions still execute, immediately observe the flag, close their intervals and report cancelled. Executing projection loops poll cooperatively, with checks around synchronous decoding and file calls. Those calls are not forcibly interrupted. A weak main-queue completion checks the request again before publishing UI. The host test suspends admission, accepts four operations, requests cancellation and resumes the queue, asserting that all four terminal callbacks drain without sleeps. The raw worker boundary may finish successfully before a later cancellation suppresses delivery, so a screen-level recorder needs a separate delivery classification. @unchecked Sendable documents the manual audit; the lock supplies synchronization.\u003c/p>",
