        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>The images array intentionally retains every successful batch UIImage while the table displays only the first. That is a strong owner, not proof of a leak. After the batch drains, Clear displayed payload removes the array's images, rows and captions. Allocation generations can show whether the corresponding application population stops surviving. Process footprint need not immediately fall because allocator pages or framework caches may remain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The probe is different: it owns a callback whose capture context strongly owns the probe. Clearing the controller's probe property removes only the external owner; the callback cycle remains. The weak witness can observe survival but does not retain the object. A stopped Memory Graph should follow incoming strong edges through the stored closure. Calling stop clears callback, breaking the cycle; the host weak-observer test verifies release when no other root exists. Neither an autorelease pool nor downsampling removes this ownership edge. I would separately inspect transient decode peak during overlap, because a small post-clear population cannot prove a safe peak on a phone.\u003c/p>",
