        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>I inventory eligible consumers with stable identities and explicit scope before counting adoption. An adopted row needs accepted behavior checks, build/target coordinates, relevant resources and interactions, and owner acceptance. Adding a dependency is a leading indicator, not consumer proof. Pending consumers and exceptions remain visible in the denominator.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The platform owner owns the schema and exception policy; consumer owners own native behavior; service and release owners preserve installed-client compatibility. I fund fixtures, adapter support, integration help, and temporary dual-path maintenance so adoption has practical incentives rather than only a mandate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An exception records its reason, owner, compensating check, baseline, and review trigger. Source deprecation warnings can start before removal, but compiler guidance does not change installed binaries. Retirement requires supported consumer evidence, compatibility review, and comparable operational outcomes. The local gate checks record completeness and attachment consistency; authentic cross-team acceptance and customer outcomes remain external evidence, never synthetic fixture achievements.\u003c/p>",
