        "androidBridge": "Media3 and Android render-thread experience suggest pipeline boundaries; Apple AVFoundation, Core Animation, Metal and Core ML still require their own evidence.",
        "rubric": [
          "Defines end-to-end intervals and a named bottleneck hypothesis.",
          "Separates media load/decode, layout/composition and ML preprocessing/prediction.",
          "Requires correctness/selection/accuracy oracles before speed claims.",
          "Explains bounded work and why main-thread GPU waits remove overlap.",
          "Provides a simpler alternative, evidence owner and rollback condition."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>Do not select a renderer from the existence of animation. Establish a reproducible interaction, representative device and intervals for clip loading/readiness, text editing/layout, preprocessing, prediction, postprocessing and visible result. Preserve selection, expected clip behavior and classifier output as correctness oracles. A measured decode delay points toward media preparation; a composition hitch points toward layer/layout work; a validated GPU workload may justify Metal.\n\nCore Animation can express ordinary geometry and opacity without a custom renderer. Metal introduces pipelines, command buffers, resource lifetime and numerical/visual validation; waitUntilCompleted in a UI frame blocks the intended CPU/GPU overlap. Core ML compute-unit configuration also does not guarantee every operation runs on a particular accelerator. Bound preparation and predictions so obsolete work cannot flood memory or publish old results. Assign the selected interval investigation to an owner and keep simpler playback/layer behavior as a rollback. Source inspection creates hypotheses; physical-device traces and unchanged correctness are required before claiming an optimization. Audio DSP, shader correctness and model evaluation remain separate specialist work.\u003c/p>",
