        "minutes": 8,
        "androidBridge": "Android/Wear permission and transport concepts are useful analogies; HealthKit intentionally hides read grants and WCSession reachability is not authorization.",
        "rubric": [
          "Separates availability, user opt-in, system authorization, execution and readiness.",
          "Explains unreachable watch queue/error recovery versus snapshot replacement.",
          "Distinguishes Bluetooth power, discovery, connection and valid payload.",
          "Preserves HealthKit read ambiguity and identifies saving-status semantics.",
          "Offers usable fallback without repeated coercive prompts."
        ],
        "answerHTML": "\u003cp>These values describe different mechanisms. A watch counterpart can be installed and activated yet unreachable for an immediate message; a queued command with stable identity can remain pending, while latest-state context uses replacement semantics. Bluetooth authorization can coexist with powered-off hardware, and a connected peripheral still needs discovered characteristics and validated payloads before its reading is useful.\n\nHealthKit adds a deliberate privacy boundary. Authorization request success does not mean all requested types were granted, and empty read results do not reveal denial. authorizationStatus(for:) describes saving authorization. Present no visible data with a manual path rather than inventing a read-denied state. Product opt-in is yet another input, distinct from system grants. A shared wrapper may expose service-specific values, but must preserve these distinctions instead of flattening them into granted. Host feature selection injects the facts and invokes none of the native services. Wear OS and Android permission experience helps ask the questions, but cannot override Apple's read-visibility or transport semantics.\u003c/p>",
        "followUps": [
          "How should old-account Bluetooth notifications be rejected?",
          "What should the UI say when health data is empty for an unknown reason?"
        ],
        "pitfalls": [
          "Treating health request completion as a read grant.",
          "Treating Bluetooth permission as proof of powered-on hardware or authenticated data."
        ]
